From 75aedbdf499c511411ecf17f806344b33749a0b4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Todd J Crane <toddjcrane@bossman.gbcyber.net>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 02:24:15 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] feat(auth): add soonest-reset routing strategy

Add a soonest-reset routing strategy (alias reset-first) that spends the
credential whose weekly usage window resets soonest, so subscription quota
that would otherwise expire unused is consumed first.

Credentials are ranked from their latest passive quota snapshot: Claude
anthropic-ratelimit-unified-5h/7d-* and Codex x-codex-primary/secondary-*
response headers, and Devin's polled daily/weekly quota. Credentials with a
used-up window go last. A Claude/Codex subscription whose weekly reset is
not known yet (after a restart or once its window rolled over) is probed
first, and concurrent picks skip it for up to five minutes while that
response is pending. Then known resets go earliest first, then other
subscriptions, then credentials with nothing to learn (API keys, other
providers). Ties resolve to the lowest ID, like fill-first.

Snapshots without usage windows, such as Claude's request-scoped fast-mode
credit refusal, keep the last observed windows, so a client that tries fast
mode before falling back does not push traffic off the soonest-reset
credential. A Claude unified rejection without windows still counts as used
up until its reset. Token counts and SelectAuth callers never spend a probe.

The scheduler fast path and the legacy selector path (used with session
affinity and by SelectAuth callers) rank the same facts: the scheduler parses
them whenever a credential's metadata is replaced, carrying them across
windowless snapshots, and the legacy path hands them to the selector through
the context, since session affinity serves most requests without a pick. The
scheduler ranks from provider-level metadata, so targeted result updates are
seen by every model shard, and the mixed-provider pick ranks across providers
like the legacy selector.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
